WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Film Courage: What elements&nbsp;
make up a great story?&nbsp;

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.880
Pat Verducci, Writing Coach, Teacher and&nbsp;
Mentor:&nbsp;Well I think when you’re writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.080
and you sort of have an idea and you go&nbsp;Wow, will&nbsp;
this actually make a great feature film or not?&nbsp;I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.800
usually think about&nbsp;Okay, are these three elements&nbsp;
part of the idea or could I find a way to sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.040
embed these three elements?&nbsp;One is that it needs&nbsp;
to have a main character who wants something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:32.240
really badly. The second one is it has to promise&nbsp;
lots of conflict. When we imagine the story and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.640
imagine what the character wants and what they’re&nbsp;
going to have to do to get it, we already start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.360
kind of imagine all the crazy obstacles that that&nbsp;
person’s going to have to face in order to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.760
what they want and then we get really interested&nbsp;
right? Then third I think a way to tell if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.480
idea will make a great movie is does it have&nbsp;
human emotions in it that we can relate to even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.840
if you're writing about science fiction or you're&nbsp;
writing a horror movie or something set way in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:06.880
past where you really have no immediate connection&nbsp;
to the time or place or characters there have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:13.360
be those human feelings and desires and fears&nbsp;
that we can relate to so I always think those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:20.640
are the three criteria that make a great movie or&nbsp;
tv idea and obviously if you're writing a tv show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.880
the idea has to promise lots of story when you're&nbsp;
writing a feature it's a contained three-act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.840
structure right so it has it's it's a contained&nbsp;
story with an end so thinking about that idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.080
all those elements still have to be at play&nbsp;
and then in addition when you're writing a tv&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:45.680
limited series or a tv show with multiple seasons&nbsp;
how are all those characters and all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:53.760
conflicts going to spool out and promise lots of&nbsp;
conflict to come yeah great great three tips first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:00.480
off how would we know that the characters want&nbsp;
is really strong enough that it warrants us being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:08.000
invested in them right well as a story consultant&nbsp;
I mean I work with a lot of screenwriters I work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.400
with professional screenwriters who are working in&nbsp;
the industry and I also work with beginning people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.360
and people who are kind of in the middle of their&nbsp;
journey toward being a professional screenwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.080
but even with the professional screenwriters that&nbsp;
I work with it's always the same it's like every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:32.320
time I read the script it's like what do they want&nbsp;
and the writer looks at me like I have no idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.840
like they've written a whole script and they're&nbsp;
just they still kind of don't understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.120
what the main character wants and so when someone&nbsp;
is thinking about their their main character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.160
I always like to break down what they want&nbsp;
into two parts like they have an emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.800
need like to find love or connection or for&nbsp;
revenge or power or whatever and then there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.600
a specific immediate goal that they pursue in&nbsp;
the story that satisfies that emotional need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.480
so if you don't have those two things and know&nbsp;
them pretty clearly you're probably going to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.600
a lot of pages that you don't end up having&nbsp;
in your final version of the script because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.000
I don't I don't know about you but I've written&nbsp;
full scripts where I literally have no idea what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.280
they want and this happens this happens this&nbsp;
happens and then I look at the script and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.480
like what is this like what is this thing why&nbsp;
should I be invested in watching this story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:33.280
so I think a way to know if you if your&nbsp;
character has enough of an engine to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.120
a feature or a television show it's like they&nbsp;
have to have a very specific emotional need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.360
and then they have a very specific goal that&nbsp;
they're pursuing in the story to satisfy that need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.960
now when they start the movie or the tv show that&nbsp;
immediate goal that they're pursuing could be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:59.520
the wrong thing right and their goal changes&nbsp;
but usually the emotional need stays constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.800
as they sort of grow then they may change&nbsp;
that that immediate goal to kind of reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:12.320
their their growth as a character or whatever but&nbsp;
like do what your character emotionally needs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:18.720
wants and then what is it that they specifically&nbsp;
decide to do to satisfy that need and once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.920
have that lined up then you're good you're good to&nbsp;
go and start writing with conflict how do we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.720
how much house like is it too much too soon or&nbsp;
there can never be too much conflict too soon like&nbsp;&nbsp;

